A||||6||TheFiero64||New Rotor Questions.||||1||
Z||000000||TheFiero64||11-08-2000||11:39 AM||||I just got new Front rotors for my 88. I am also installing new pads and greasing my sliders. What is the proper rotor break-in procedure and how can I keep the rotor from getting rust on it? Thanks in advance.<P>------------------<BR>88 Coupe V6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Old Lar||11-08-2000||12:03 PM||||If you don't want rust on the rotor don't drive the car in wet weather or expose it to humid weather. Most rotors rust. Since the material they are made from contains the element iron which when oxidizes forms iron oxide which you call rust.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||william_thorniii||11-08-2000||12:07 PM||||64,<P>Most brake manufacturers reccomend completely stopping the car from 20 mph at least 3 times before normal braking is performed.  To the best of my knowledge this is ONLY required when after replacing either rotors or pads, not when both should have a flat surface.  So in my opinion, you can do this to be safe, but it should not be necessary in your situation.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
Z||000003||1FST2M6||11-08-2000||12:08 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> you can do like brembo does on many of theier cross drilled and slotted rotors and have the iridium coated. or cadmium coated. but that only lasts a short while.. or you can get stainless discs. they are pricey but last longer and will be REALLY difficult to warp...  or carbon fiber! KVR makes a kit for the GS-R and Civic with Carbon fiber discs and Brembo make a kit for the mustang too! but at $160,000 a corner may seem steep. it was in an old Mustang Monthly the 1000rwhp street cobra has a set.. the ONLY set they have ever sold! hmmm wonder why.. McLaren uses them as does Ferrari and Mercedes. Panoz uses stainless and titanium||||reg||1
Z||000004||lawrence||11-08-2000||12:23 PM||||As Old Lar pointed out, you're not likely to keep them from rusting, especially inside the vane area.  You can, however, improve the appearance of the rotors by painting them with Rustoleum.  I did this on my car.<P>After you wash the car, the rotors will almost immediately develop a surface coat of rust (assuming you removed the oily coating that the rotors came with).  Drive the car around the block, and that will clean the surface where the pads rub the rotors.<P>Then, remove the wheels and brush on some Rustoleum.  I use silver, but you can do what you want.  Try not to get any paint on the surface where the pad meets the rotor, but if you do, don't worry too much.  It will abraid off very quickly.<P> <A HREF="http://www.webelectricmagazine.com/99/4/rotors.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.webelectricmagazine.com/99/4/rotors.htm</A>  is a shot of my rotors before and after.  The one on the left is the old rotor.  You will see the 1/8" ring of paint on the outside of the rotor.  I hadn't used these rotors in a while, so the pad area had already started to rust (dark color).  Also note that since I painted these after they had already started to rust, the paint tends to flake off as they continue to rust.<P>The rotor on the right is the new one.  Pinting it when it was new has reduced the flaking considerably.  There is no outside ring of paint on the rotor because I mounted the caliper at the very edge of it.  You can see the paint on the inside of the rotor surface, and on the hat.<P>I'm a fanatic when it comes to brakes, which means I have the wheels off at least once a summer.  When this happens, I give the rotors a quick brush-up and they look nice through the black wheels as you can see here: <A HREF="http://www.webelectricmagazine.com/99/4/wheel.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://www.webelectricmagazine.com/99/4/wheel.jpg</A> <P>Good luck,<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://www.webelectricmagazine.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.webelectricmagazine.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||TheFiero64||11-08-2000||12:51 PM||||Thanks for all the tips. I will use a paint on them. I hate the rusty look.<P>------------------<BR>88 Coupe V6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif"> ||||reg||10
Z||000006||theogre||11-08-2000||07:30 PM||||Don't paint anything but the hub portion. The outer edge will just flake off like lawrence said.<P>With new parts there isn't much breakin required. <P>The most critical thing is make sure everything is perfectly clean. Any oil or storage coating at all left on the rotors will cause trouble. I clean new rotors with carb cleaner or gasket remover to eat off anything on them then clean them with brake cleaner to make sure there is no residue.||||reg||1
